   "summary": "Unlock these with Subscription The All India Institute of Medical Sciences (AIIMS), Delhi has performed India’s first open foetal repair of a lumbosacral myelomeningocele — the most severe form of spina bifida — at 25 weeks’ gestation. Also read | AIIMS surgery department marks 1,000 robotic operations milestone “The six-hour operation repaired the cerebrospinal-fluid-leaking lumbosacral defect in utero. The patient was first screened and counselled by K.",
   "summary_zh": "订阅即可解锁。印度全印医学科学研究所（AIIMS）德里分院在妊娠第25周完成了印度首例开放式胎儿 lumbosacral 脊髓脊膜膨出修复术——这是脊柱裂最严重的一种形式。另请阅读｜AIIMS外科部门迎来机器人手术1000例里程碑。历时6小时的手术在宫内修复了脑脊液渗漏的腰骶部缺损。患者最初由K.进行筛查和咨询……",
   "summarySource": "fulltext",
   "body": "Account subscription benefits alongside Premium Stories, Editorials, Opinions and more. Unlock these with Subscription The All India Institute of Medical Sciences (AIIMS), Delhi has performed India’s first open foetal repair of a lumbosacral myelomeningocele — the most severe form of spina bifida — at 25 weeks’ gestation. Also read | AIIMS surgery department marks 1,000 robotic operations milestone “The six-hour operation repaired the cerebrospinal-fluid-leaking lumbosacral defect in utero. It followed more than five weeks of multidisciplinary planning, detailed foetal imaging, non-directive counselling, anaesthetic preparation, and operative simulation,’’ AIIMS said in a statement. A myelomeningocele is an open neural tube defect in which the spinal cord and its coverings protrude through an opening in the spine. The patient was first screened and counselled by K. Aparna Sharma from the Maternal–Fetal Medicine team. The multidisciplinary procedure was performed using high-end microscopy and continuous high-end ultrasonography assistance at the Neurosciences Centre. The procedure was provided free of cost to the family under the Union government’s Janani Shishu Suraksha Karyakram (JSSK). Deepak Gupta, who led the neurosurgical component, said that the intervention was done after rigorous selection. “Prenatal repair seeks to limit ongoing injury to exposed neural tissue, reduce the subsequent need for cerebrospinal-fluid diversion for hydrocephalus, and improve selected neurological outcomes. It is not a cure, and long-term multidisciplinary care remains essential,” he said. More than 30 clinicians and nurses from the Obstetrics and Gynaecology, Maternal–Fetal Medicine, Neurosurgery, Obstetrics and Neuroanaesthesia, Plastic Surgery, Radiodiagnosis, Neonatology, and Nursing teams of the Neurosciences Centre and Obstetrics–Gynaecology services participated in the procedure. Professor Aparna Sharma added that this foetal intervention was well-reasoned and a planned decision aimed to optimise the outcomes of both the baby and the mother. The safety of both mother and foetus was ensured through detailed counselling, high-quality anaesthesia, and coordinated surveillance before and after surgery. “International trial evidence supports prenatal repair in carefully selected foetuses with open myelomeningocele. The procedure nevertheless carries important maternal and foetal risks, including prematurity and uterine-scar complications, and must be performed only in specialised centres,’’ she said. The team emphasised that foetal surgery is a treatment for selected pregnancies, not a substitute for prevention. Neural-tube closure occurs by approximately 28 days after conception, often before pregnancy is recognised. Pre-conception folic-acid counselling, evidence-based food fortification with folates, and robust national surveillance remain essential to reducing India’s burden of neural tube defects. “The mother and foetus will continue to receive close multidisciplinary surveillance through pregnancy, delivery, and the newborn period,’’ the AIIMS statement said, adding that a child with spina bifida may begin life with paralysis, hydrocephalus, and lifelong challenges. Traditionally, neurosurgeons have done this operation after the child is born. But by then, some of the neurological damage may already have occurred. For some children with spina bifida, the opportunity to protect the spinal cord now begins before birth. Comments have to be in English, and in full sentences. They cannot be abusive or personal. Please abide by our community guidelines for posting your comments. We have migrated to a new commenting platform. If you are already a registered user of The Hindu and logged in, you may continue to engage with our articles. If you do not have an account please register and login to post comments. Users can access their older comments by logging into their accounts on Vuukle",
